
// draft
// The element type is generic in the library; Int stands in for it here.
// n below 1 makes the library raise (islice refuses the step), so no output satisfies these
// sentences there.
// Author to decide: n as an error case in a Result, or a precondition. Not drafted: the problem
// names no error.
chunked_even(iterable: Int[], n: Int) -> Int[][]
	note "break the list into the fewest chunks of at most n items, in order, with chunk lengths differing by at most one"
	# The chunks, joined in order, are the input list.
	flatten(out) == iterable
	# Every chunk has at most n items.
	all c in out: len(c) <= n
	# Any two chunks' lengths differ by at most one.
	all a in out: all b in out: abs(len(a) - len(b)) <= 1
	# One fewer chunk than the output has, at n items each, could not hold the input: the output's
	# chunk count minus one, times n, is less than the input's length.
	(len(out) - 1) * n < len(iterable)
	# No chunk is longer than the chunk before it.
	all a in out: all b in out where a before b in out: len(b) <= len(a)
	// example wanted: the docstring case, [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7] at n 3, where the balance moves one
	// item off the first chunk compared with plain chunking
	// example wanted: an empty input at n 3; the count bound alone decides how many chunks there
	// are
	// example wanted: an input whose length is an exact multiple of n, such as six items at n 3,
	// where every chunk is full
	// example wanted: fewer items than n, such as two items at n 5; the count bound allows only one
	// chunk
	// example wanted: n of 1, where every chunk holds a single item
	// example wanted: a long input, such as 23 items at n 4, where the library yields full chunks
	// of n while streaming before it balances the remainder; the lengths are still decided by the
	// balance and count sentences
